# Confined Synthesis of 2D Nanostructured Materials toward Electrocatalysis

## abstract
2D nanostructured materials have shown great application prospects in energy conversion, owing to their unique structural features and fascinating physicochemical properties. Developing efficient approaches for the synthesis of well‐defined 2D nanostructured materials with controllable composition and morphology is critical. The emerging concept, confined synthesis, has been regarded as a promising strategy to design and synthesize novel 2D nanostructured materials. This review mainly summarizes the recent advances in confined synthesis of 2D nanostructured materials by using layered materials as host matrices (also denoted as "nanoreactors"). By virtue of the space‐ and surface‐confinement effects of these layered hosts, various well‐organized 2D nanostructured materials, including 2D metals, 2D metal compounds, 2D carbon materials, 2D polymers, 2D metal‐organic frameworks (MOFs) and covalent‐organic frameworks (COFs), as well as 2D carbon nitrides are successfully synthesized. The wide employment of these 2D materials in electrocatalytic applications (e.g., electrochemical oxygen/hydrogen evolution reactions, small molecule oxidation, and oxygen reduction reaction) is presented and discussed. In the final section, challenges and prospects in 2D confined synthesis from the viewpoint of designing new materials and exploring practical applications are commented, which would push this fast‐evolving field a step further toward greater success in both fundamental studies and ultimate industrialization.

2D nanostructured materials have shown great application prospects in energy conversion, owing to their unique structural features and fascinating physicochemical properties. Developing efficient approaches for the synthesis of well-defined 2D nanostructured materials with controllable composition and morphology is critical. The emerging concept, confined synthesis, has been regarded as a promising strategy to design and synthesize novel 2D nanostructured materials. This review mainly summarizes the recent advances in confined synthesis of 2D nanostructured materials by using layered materials as host matrices (also denoted as “nanoreactors”). By virtue of the space- and surface-confinement effects of these layered hosts, various well-organized 2D nanostructured materials, including 2D metals, 2D metal compounds, 2D carbon materials, 2D polymers, 2D metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and covalent-organic frameworks (COFs), as well as 2D carbon nitrides are successfully synthesized. The wide employment of these 2D materials in electrocatalytic applications (e.g., electrochemical oxygen/hydrogen evolution reactions, small molecule oxidation, and oxygen reduction reaction) is presented and discussed. In the final section, challenges and prospects in 2D confined synthesis from the viewpoint of designing new materials and exploring practical applications are commented, which would push this fast-evolving field a step further toward greater success in both fundamental studies and ultimate industrialization.
## 1. Introduction
The design and preparation of well-defined nanomaterials with precise control over their structures and compositions are extremely important for tuning their physicochemical properties and functionalities.[1–3] Up to date, extensive methods have been adopted for the preparation of novel nanostructures, such as the wet-chemical synthesis,[4,5] electrochemical synthesis,[6,7] chemical vapor deposition (CVD),[8,9] and template-assisted synthesis.[10,11] Among them, the template-assisted synthesis strategy has been widely used to control the morphologies and compositions of nanomaterials.[12–14] Nevertheless, how to obtain precisely controlled nanostructures remains a challenge. To solve this problem, confined synthesis method, as an extension of the template-assisted synthesis method, has been proposed. Confinement, a concept coming from catalysis and surface science, has been utilized for the restriction of nanoparticles or molecules in a nanospace or on the surface to improve their catalytic properties.[15–20] In the field of materials science and engineering, the concept of confinement has also been developed for the controlled synthesis of nanomaterials. The template used for the confined synthesis not only serves as a physical reactor to define the shape and size of nanomaterials, but also provides specific chemical microenvironment for the confined nucleation, growth and stabilization of intermediates or products.
2D nanomaterials have shown great application prospect in energy-related fields owing to their unique physicochemical properties and thus attracted considerable research interest.[21–24] A variety of synthetic strategies, including topdown methods via the mechanical or liquid exfoliation,[25–27] and bottom-up methods via the wet-chemical synthesis or CVD,[28–30] have been adopted for the preparation of 2D materials. However, these methods may suffer from harsh synthetic conditions, e.g., high temperature or high vacuum, low yield, or poor stability of products, etc. Alternatively, the directed synthesis of well-organized layered or nonlayered 2D derivatives can be achieved by capitalizing on the layered materials, which function as host matrices (so-called “2D nanoreactors”). For instance, graphite,[31,32] layered double hydroxides (LDHs),[33,34] silicate (magadiite, montmorillonite (MMT)),[35,36] MXenes,[37,38] and $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}^{[39,40]}$ have been recognized as effective hosts for confined synthesis. Reagents are confined due to the open 2D channels and the presence of diverse functional groups on the surface, forming products with limited dimensions and unique properties. In addition, the thermal stability and mechanical robustness of the layered matrix allow the synthesis to be carried out under harsh conditions.
Herein, we present the concept for the synthesis of welldefined 2D nanomaterials based on the confinement effect. Then, the confined synthesis of 2D nanostructured materials, including 2D metals, 2D metal compounds, 2D carbon materials, as well as other materials (2D polymers, 2D MOFs and COFs, 2D carbon nitride, and porous clay heterostructures) is comprehensively summarized. After that, we highlight the advantages of these well-defined ultrathin nanomaterials in electrocatalytic applications, including electrocatalytic oxidation reactions (oxygen evolution reaction and small molecule oxidation) and electrocatalytic reduction reactions (e.g., hydrogen evolution reaction, oxygen reduction reaction). At last, we make a detailed summary about the challenges and perspectives for the confined synthesis of 2D nanostructured materials based on the current research achievements.
## 2. Confined Synthesis
Based on the structural features of layered materials and the synthetic conditions, the confined synthesis can be generally classified into “space-confined synthesis” and “surface-confined synthesis” (Scheme  1). The space-confined synthesis refers to confining the reactants into the interlayer spaces of layered materials to direct the growth of nanomaterials. For instance, the layered materials (e.g., LDHs, MMT, magadiite, graphene, and MXenes) can provide limited spaces for the embedded guest molecules, in which a chemical transformation would occur under proper conditions. In addition, surface-confined synthesis has recently attracted intensive attention in surface chemistry.[41–43] In this method, featuring the abundant exposed surface atoms, surface defects and surface functional groups possessed by the 2D nanomaterials, the reactants, intermediates, and products can be adsorbed on their solid surface while retaining certain mobility. The target materials are obtained based on the surface-confinement effect of the host matrix. Because the synthetic process is carried out in the 2D space or on the 2D surface, the resulting materials can preserve good 2D nanostructures.
## 3. 2D Nanostructured Materials Based on Confined Synthesis
