## 3.1. Confined Synthesis of 2D Metals
2D metal nanomaterials usually exhibit extraordinary catalytic performances compared to their bulk counterparts and nanomaterials in other dimensionalities, attributed to the largely exposed surface with abundant active sites and unique surface coordination environment.[44–46] Unfortunately, it is still a challenge to prepare well-organized and stable 2D metal nanomaterials on a large scale via traditional wet-chemical synthesis method. With the assistance of 2D confined synthesis strategy, well-defined ultrathin metal nanosheets (NSs) have been successfully synthesized by using layered hosts as nanoreactors. For example, Xiao and co-workers synthesized ultrathin 2D Au NSs within the interlayer of LDH by in situ reducing the intercalated Au precursors (Figure  1a).[47] The high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) image of a single Au NS and the corresponding fast Fourier transform (FFT) pattern indicated that the Au NS was single crystalline and grew in the [001] direction (Figure  1b,c). This is due to the preferential interaction between the Au (001) surface and the positively charged LDH laminates. The Au NS has three atomic layers and its lateral boundaries mainly comprise $\{100\}$ -type zigzag edges. In addition, Tin (Sn) is one of the most attractive anode materials for lithium ion batteries. However, due to the low melting point of $\mathrm{Sn}$ , it is hard to obtain a sheet-like structure by a normal preparation process. Graphene was utilized as a nanoreactor for the successfully synthesis of $\mathrm{sn}$ nanosheet via space-confined synthesis method.[48] Briefly, $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ ions were first adsorbed on graphene oxide (GO) NSs through the electrostatic interaction by slowly adding polyvinyl alcohol and $\mathrm{\SnCl_{4}}$ into GO aqueous solution. Then, $\mathrm{GO}@\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}$ membrane was fabricated via a simple filtration process. Metallic Sn NSs were ultimately obtained through the in situ reduction of $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ ions by carbon in the interlayer of GO during thermal treatment at high temperature (Figure  1d). The GO NSs could direct the growth of Sn NSs and restrict their aggregation. The obtained graphene/Sn NSs exhibit a sandwich structure, which not only provides a convenient ion/electron transmission pathway but also accommodates the volume changes of Sn during electrochemical processes. In addition, the monolayer graphene can be heavily oxidized with the presence of diverse oxygen-containing functional groups (e.g., $-\mathrm{COOH}$ and $-\mathrm{OH}^{\prime}$ , offering sufficient anchoring sites for the growth of 2D noble metals.[49,50] Recently, Zhang and co-workers successfully synthesized Au square sheets on GO surface (Figure  1e).[51] The Au nanosheets were synthesized by adding $4\times10^{-3}$ m of $\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}$ ,
$140\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~}$ of 1-amino-9-octadecene and GO NSs in a mixture of hexane and ethanol, followed by heat treatment at $55~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{16\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The as-obtained Au nanosheets showed an ultrathin thickness of ${\approx}2.4~\mathrm{nm}$ (corresponding to approximately $16~\mathrm{Au}$ atomic layers) and an edge length of $200{-}500\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Interestingly, the obtained Au nanosheets possessed an unusual hexagonal close-packed crystal phase and the ABAB stacking could be viewed along the $[001]_{h}$ direction. Moreover, based on the surface confinement effect of $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ NSc, the same group also reported the synthesis of Ag nanoplates on the surface of single-layer $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ NSs.[52] In this 2D ${\mathrm{Ag}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{MoS}}_{2}$ composite, the {100} plane of $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ was aligned with the 1/3{422} plane of the surface-grown Ag nanoplate with an angular misorientation of ${\approx}6^{\circ}$ , indicating an epitaxial relationship.
## 3.2. Confined Synthesis of 2D Metal Compounds
Atomically thin 2D inorganic materials, especially transitionmetal oxides (TMOs) and transition-metal dichalcogenides (TMDs), have gained significant interest because of their unique structures and promising energy-related applications.[53–56] Recently, the confined synthesis has been widely used for the preparation of 2D TMOs and TMDs by using layered materials to disperse the precursors and assist the nucleation and subsequent crystallization. For example, Xie and co-workers synthesized graphene-based 2D vanadium oxide superlattice NSs in the interlayer region of reduced graphene oxide (rGO) via space-confined synthesis method. In their work, the graphene NSs served as both space-confined nanoreactor and electron donor to direct the growth of monolayer vanadium oxide NSs with a highly symmetrical framework.[57] The synthetic process is schematically illustrated in Figure  2a. Typically, ${\mathrm{VO}}({\mathrm{acac}})_{2}$ was first hydrolyzed to the positively charged $\mathrm{|VO(H}_{2}O)_{5}]^{2+}$ in water. Then the $\mathrm{|VO(H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{5}]^{2+}$ was intercalated between the rGO NSs and transformed to $\mathrm{vo}_{6}$ octahedra. Finally, vanadium oxide NSs were produced in the lamella space of rGO. The vanadium oxide NSs showed a remarkably different local atomic arrangement with a more symmetric V–O coordination as compared with the bulk $\mathrm{VO}_{2}$ counterpart, demonstrating the formation of superlattice NSs of vanadium oxide. Besides, the vanadium oxide NSs showed structural rearrangements due to the stress field and electron transfer at the interface between rGO and vanadium oxide, which endowed the NSs with magnetic property. Similarly, Golberg and co-workers synthesized $\alpha{\cdot}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ hexagonal nanoplatelets within the interlayer of functionalized graphene NSs.[58] In their work, 7,7,8,8-tetracyanoquinodimethane $\mathrm{TCNQ^{-}}$ ions-intercalated graphene NSs were used to absorb $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ ions into the graphene sandwich layers. Then, nucleation and growth of single crystal $\alpha{\cdot}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ hexa­ gonal platelets occurred along the six equivalent ${<}110>$ directions between graphene layers. The $\mathrm{TCNQ^{-}}$ ions played an important role in assisting the growth of $\alpha{\cdot}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanoplatelets and ensuring the production of N-doped graphene, which promoted electrochemical performance of the resulting material. It should be noted that the most common structure of $\alpha{\cdot}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is rhombohedral, thus an epitaxial growth of $\alpha{\cdot}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ is very difficult owing to the rather close surface energies of different facets. The confined synthesis method in this work provides an effective way to obtain 2D $\alpha{\cdot}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ nanosheets with welldefined hexagonal shape. In addition, Hu and co-workers successfully synthesized defect-rich $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ NSs within graphene nano­reactor by intercalating sodium molybdate and thiourea into the lamella region of $\mathrm{rGO}$ (Figure 2b).[59] The oxygen-containing groups on rGO NSs induced strong adsorption of the precursors into the interlayer space of $\mathtt{r G O}$ , which facilitated the nucleation and growth of $\mathbf{MoS}_{2}$ NSs in such a confined environment. More recently, Fang and co-workers found that the 2D NaCl crystals with unconventional stoichiometries (i.e., $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}$ and $\mathrm{Na}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}_{\mathrm{\ell}}^{\mathrm{\prime}}$ can be obtained in the interlayer region of rGO (Figure  2c).[60] The formation of 2D NaCl crystals was confirmed by X-ray diffraction (XRD), from which a new Bragg peak at ${\approx}32^{\circ}$ is observed (Figure 2d). This new 2D NaCl crystal is rather different from the traditional $3\mathrm{D\NaCl}$ crystal, which is attributed to the cation– $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\pi$ interaction between the ions and the aromatic rings in the graphitic surface.
## 3.3. Confined Synthesis of 2D Carbon Materials
Nanostructured carbons, such as 0D fullerenes,[61] 1D carbon nanotubes,[62] and 2D graphene,[63] have shown intriguing physicochemical properties due to the unique $\displaystyle\mathtt{s p}^{2}$ hybrid atom structure of these carbon materials. Along with the rapid development of graphene and its derivatives, 2D carbonbased nanomaterials have attracted tremendous research interest.[64,65] Up to now, a variety of interesting 2D carbonbased nanocomposites have been synthesized based on the confined synthesis method. Generally, the 2D carbon-based materials are derived from the organic molecule precursors, which are first intercalated into the 2D interlayer region of layered materials, and then carbonized under inert atmosphere. For instance, Lu and co-workers synthesized singlelayered graphene quantum dots (S-GQDs) by using LDHs as the space-confined nanoreactor (Figure  3a).[66] The well-defined S-GQDs were synthesized via introducing citrate into the interlayer region of MgAl-LDH, followed by a hydrothermal carbonization process in the presence of ammonia at high temperature. The as-produced S-GQDs were loaded into the interlayer of LDHs with a size distribution of $2.2\pm0.2$ nm and a single-layered thickness of $0.7~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure  3b). Similarly, Yang and co-workers synthesized graphene NSs by co-intercalating dodecyl sulfonate and methyl methacrylate into the 2D interlayer of MgAl-LDH, followed with a pyrolysis process in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere.[67] The thickness of the as-formed graphene NSs can be tuned from monolayer to multilayers by changing the amount of methyl methacrylate precursor embedded in the LDH. Dai and co-workers synthesized carbon NSs with a high surface area by using a layered material (i.e., magadiite) as the nanoreactor and metal halideionic liquids as the carbon precursor. Briefly, the metal salts (i.e., $\mathrm{{MnCl}}_{2}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{NiCl}_{2}-$ , $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{}^{-}$ , or $\mathrm{CoCl}_{2}^{-}$ ) containing Bmim[Br] eutectic liquid were first intercalated into the interlayer of magadiite through ultrasonication. Then, porous carbon NSs were obtained via pyrolyzing the composite at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ followed by an ammonium bifluoride etching process to remove the silicate substrate.[68] In addition, MXenes,[69–71] a class of typical layered materials, can also be employed as the nanoreactor for confined synthesis of 2D carbon materials. More recently, Yamauchi and co-workers synthesized 2D porous carbon with a layerby-layer motif architecture by introducing amphiphilic triblock copolymer F127 (PEO-PPO-PEO) unimers and phenolic resol molecules into the lamella region of MXene NSs, which were then converted to ordered mesoporous carbon layers after a thermal treatment process in an inert atmosphere. Finally, all-carbon 2D–2D heterostructured material was obtained by removing the metal elements from MXene (Figure 3c).[72] The ordered mesoporous carbon layers were intercalated within the MXene-derived carbon layers, preventing the restacking of NSs and providing accessible pathways for ion diffusion and electron transfer (Figure 3d). This facile method is promising for the large-scale synthesis of highly dispersed carbon NSs and the fabrication of energy conversion and storage devices.
In addition to the space-confined method, the nanostructured carbon materials can also be obtained via the surfaceconfined approach. For example, Shao and co-workers reported the synthesis of 2D porous carbon nanomaterials through surface-confined growth of zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) on the surface of CoAl-LDH, followed by a carbonization process (Figure  3e).[73] The as-prepared ZIF-67 crystals were uniformly grown on the surface of CoAl-LDHs due to the metal-imidazole coordination effect, which promoted the structural transformation from ZIF-67 to carbon networks. As shown in Figure  3f, the resulting carbon-based network showed a typical 2D porous structure. It was found that the concentration of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ in the LDH laminates could greatly influence the growth of ZIF-67 microcrystals and the final carbonbased nanostructures. Similarly, Wu and co-workers prepared ordered mesoporous carbon NSs by using LDHs as a surfaceconfined nanoreactor.[74] The surface of MgAl-LDH plays a crucial role in tuning the orientation of mesopores in the carbon NSs, where vertically or horizontally aligned mesopores can be obtained. Moreover, various transition metal elements, such as Fe, Ni, Co, Cu, Mn, and V, can be introduced and well-dispersed in the host layers of LDHs.[33,34] These transition metals in the LDH laminates can be converted to highly dispersed metal oxides and metallic nanoparticles through calcination and reduction processes,[75] which can be used for the growth of carbon nanomaterials. Based on this route, several carbon materials, such as 2D graphene framework and CNT arrays, have been synthesized by using LDHs as both catalyst and surface-confined nanoreactor.[76–79] For example, based on the surface-confinement effect of LDHs, different kinds of CNT array-assembled nanosheets were synthesized by our group through an interesting atom-economically self-sustaining CVD method.[78,79] In these materials, N-doped CNTs with a diameter of ${\approx}11$ nm and a length of several hundred nanometers were vertically grafted on both sides of the LDH-derived nano­ platelets, resulting in a hierarchical cactus-like morphology. These nanocarbon materials show excellent catalytic performances in various electrocatalytic reactions, including oxygen reduction and water splitting.
## 3.4. Confined Synthesis of Other 2D Materials
2D polymers: Artificial design and preparation of polymers into a 2D nanostructure is captivating in terms of their controllable and fascinating molecular arrangement, crystal structures, and physicochemical properties.[80,81] Space-confined synthesis has been widely used to prepare 2D polymers by intercalating organic monomers into the interlayer of layered materials, followed by an in situ polymerization reaction. MXenes have been successfully used as nanoreactor to synthesize 2D conductive polymers. As shown in Figure  4a, Gogotsi and co-workers demonstrated the space-confined synthesis of polypyrrole (PPy) within MXene $(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x})$ by mixing a colloidal solution of delaminated $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ suspension with pyrrole.[82] The PPy chains were located in the interlayers of MXene with a well-aligned arrangement. Recently, $\mathtt{X u}$ and co-workers also synthesized ultrathin PPy NSs via space-confined synthesis method by using iron oxychloride (FeOCl), a layered inorganic solid, as nanoreactor.[83] As schematically illustrated in Figure  4b, the pyrrole monomer was intercalated into the van der Waals gap of FeOCl, followed by an in situ polymerization process to form PPy. Then ultrathin PPy NSs were obtained by removing the FeOCl template via a facile acid washing treatment. Moreover, this strategy can be extended to the synthesis of other 2D polymers by changing the host and guest molecules.[84–86] For example, Wang and co-workers produced 2D MgAl-LDH-polyacrylic acid nanohybrid by intercalating acrylate monomer into MgAl-LDH, followed by an in situ polymerization reaction with potassium persulfate as the initiator.[84] Similarly, Leroux and co-workers synthesized 2D LDH-poly(sodium- $p$ -styrenesulfonate) nano­ hybrid by intercalating vinylbenzene sulfonate into ZnAl-LDH through ion-exchange, followed by an in situ polymerization process in the interlayer of LDH at 180 °C.[85]
2D MOFs and COFs: Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a promising class of nanoporous materials.[87–89] Recently, 2D MOFs have attracted considerable interest in the electrocatalysis field due to their porous structure, which can efficiently expose active metal centers and promote fast mass/ion transport.[90,91] Conventionally, 2D MOF nanosheets can be prepared by various methods, such as liquid exfoliation,[92–95] layer-by-layer growth,[96–98] and surfactant-assisted synthesis.[99,100] Alternatively, 2D confined synthesis provides a new approach to prepare 2D MOFs. For example, Loh and co-workers successfully synthesized 2D graphene-metalloporphyrin MOF via the reaction between pyridine-functionalized graphene and iron-porphyrin (Figure 5a).[101] It was found that the crystallization of iron-porphyrin in the MOF was affected by the pyridine-functionalized graphene. The as-obtained 2D MOF exhibited an enhanced electrochemical charge transfer rate and thus showed a good activity toward the electrochemical oxygen reduction reaction. The same group reported that the benzoic acid-functionalized graphene sheets can also influence the crystal growth of MOFs.[102] More recently, Huang and co-workers prepared a new MXene-MOF composite via surface-confined synthesis of 2D Co-based MOFs on MXene $(\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{x})$ NSs.[103] As illustrated in Figure  5b, ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions were first absorbed on $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$
NSs via electrostatic interaction with $-\mathrm{F}_{x}$ and $-(\mathrm{OH})_{x}$ on the surface of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ NSs. Then, the surface-anchored ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ ions were coordinated with 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (BDC) mole­ cules to form the $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ -CoBDC hybrid. The obtained hybrid material showed an excellent activity toward electrochemical water splitting. In addition to the confined synthesis of 2D MOFs, recently, Liu and co-workers reported the surface-confined synthesis of atomic thick 2D covalent-organic frameworks (COFs) on single-layer graphene.[43] Briefly, $p$ -phenylenediamine (PDA) and benzene-1,3,5-tricarbaldehyde (BTA) were first mixed in octanoic acid. Then a small amount of the mixing solution with BTA and PDA mole ratio of 1:3 was drop-casted on graphene at room temperature. The BTA and PDA were selfassembled to form 2D COF in several minutes. In addition, the authors used scanning tunneling microscopy to monitor the surface-confined synthesis process of COF. Unlike insulating MOF or COF materials, 2D MOFs and COFs prepared by the confined synthesis method possess new electrochemical properties, which show promising applications in the fields of electrocatalysis, electrochemical energy storage, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ storage, and photoelectric chemistry.[104–106]
2D carbon nitrides: Carbon nitride, with a layered structure analogous to graphite, has been proven to be an excellent photo-/ thermal-active material for photocatalytic water splitting, bioimaging and pollutant degradation, etc.[107–109] However, pristine carbon nitride is a typical bulk material that is prepared at high temperatures $(500-600^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , causing low surface area and sluggish reaction kinetics.[107] How to construct 2D carbon nitride is of high importance to enlarge the surface area and optimize the active sites, so as to achieve performance enhancement. Recently, Wei and co-workers successfully synthesized ultrathin carbon nitride in LDHs via space-confined synthesis method (Figure  6a).[109] Citric acid (CA)-intercalated MgAl-LDH nanoplatelets (CA/LDH, diameter of ${\approx}60~\mathrm{nm}$ ; Figure  6b) were first prepared through separate nucleation and ageing steps. Then the CA/LDH precursor was dispersed in a urea aqueous solution and heated in a microwave reactor, generating ultrathin carbon nitride in the confined region of LDH matrix.
The CA/LDH changed from white to yellow color with a sheetlike morphology but a slightly increased size (Figure 6c), demonstrating the successful production of ultrathin carbon nitride, which were further verified by the XRD pattern and HRTEM images. The host–guest interactions between the carbon nitride and LDH contribute to the recombination of photoexcited electron–hole pairs, resulting in an extremely high solid-state quantum yield.
2D porous clay heterostructures: Another type of 2D nanostructured materials that are obtained through confined synthesis based on the layered clays (e.g., magadiite, MMT) are called porous clay heterostructures (PCHs). PCHs, a kind of pillared interlayered clays, first reported by Galarneau and co-workers in 1995,[110] possess a unique micro/mesoporous structure with large surface area and enhanced solid acidity. Typically, long chain alkyl ammonium cations (CTMABr) were first intercalated into the interlayer of magadiite via cation exchange reaction to expand the interlayer spacing. Then the neutral amine cosurfactant molecules along with the silica precursor (such as tetraethylorthosilicate (TEOS)) were further introduced into the gallery of magadiite, followed by in situ hydrolysis and polymerization processes. PCH sample with porous network structure was finally produced by removing the organic surfactants through calcination (Figure 6d).[111] The ordered 2D layered structure of the PCH was observed from the HRTEM image (Figure  6e), which showed an interplanar spacing of $3.13~\mathrm{nm}$ at the curly edges, corresponding to the (001) plane of PCH.[112] The unique structural features make PCHs promising candidates as adsorbents, catalysts, carriers, and templates.
## 4. Electrocatalysis Application of 2D Nanomaterials
## 4.1. Electrocatalytic Oxidation Reactions
Electrocatalytic oxidation reactions, including oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and small molecules oxidation, play important roles in many energy conversion systems.[6] The development of highly efficient electrocatalysts with highly active reaction sites, highly exposed electrochemically active surface area and an optimal electron transfer structure is important and has attracted considerable attention.[113–115] Among different reported OER catalysts, LDHs, especially NiFe-LDH, have been proven to be promising catalysts in alkaline solutions.[116,117] However, their OER performance is still limited by the poor electronic conductivity and limited active sites. Taking the advantage of surface-confined synthesis of 2D porous carbon nanoplates, Zhang and co-workers demonstrated that the composite consisting of NiFe-LDHs and graphene can be used as a high-performance OER electrocatalyst.[118] As schematically illustrated in Figure  7a, NiFe-LDHs with nanometer size (nNiFe-LDHs) were synthesized on the porous surface of the nitrogen-doped graphene framework (NGF). The nitrogen dopant and topology-induced defects of NGF provided abundant adsorption sites for the anchoring and growth of nNiFeLDHs. The nNiFe-LDHs showed excellent OER performances under alkaline conditions with a remarkably decreased overpotential ( $337~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ ) and a low Tafel slope $(45\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}})$ ), superior to those of bulk NiFe-LDH $(459~\mathrm{mV};$ $62\ \mathrm{mV\dec^{-1}}$ ), NGF $(615\mathrm{~mV;~}302\mathrm{~mV~dec^{-1})}$ , and $\mathrm{Ir/C}$ catalyst $(410\ \mathrm{mV};54\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ (Figure 7b). The good OER activity was attributed to the unique structural features of this hierarchical structure: 1) the highly conductive and mesoporous NGF scaffold afforded a facile electron transfer pathway;
2) the nNiFe-LDHs, as the reactive species, effectively modified the surface character and produced highly active sites; 3) nitrogen incorporated into the carbon frameworks could tailor the electronic structure of this hybrid. Similarly, Qiu and co-workers present a thermally exfoliated graphene (TEG) confined strategy to construct active edge site-enriched CoFe-LDHs nanosheets for OER.[119] The as-obtained CoFe-LDH/TEG nanohybrids exhibited an overpotential of $301~\mathrm{mV}$ at the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and Tafel slope of $52~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{dec^{-1}}$ , much lower than that of pure CoFe-LDH $(342~\mathrm{mV;~}80~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}})$ . In addition, the OER performance of NiFe-LDH can be further enhanced by decorating single Au atoms on the surface of LDH through surface-confined synthesis strategy (Figure 7c).[120] The single Au/NiFe-LDH catalyst showed a sixfold enhancement in OER activity than that of NiFe-LDH with a low overpotential of $237~\mathrm{mV}$ at the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . It was found that the single Au atoms on NiFe oxyhydroxide play an important role in inducing the charge redistribution of active Fe as well as its surrounding atoms. Moreover, Wang and coworkers demonstrated that molybdenum oxide-decorated NiFe alloy NSs (NiFe- $.\mathrm{MoO}_{x}$ NSs) acted as a high-performance OER catalyst.[121] 2D NiFe- $.\mathrm{MoO}_{x}$ NSs were prepared via intercalating $\mathrm{MoO}_{4}{}^{2-}$ into NiFe-LDHs followed by a reduction process. The as-obtained 2D NiFe- $\mathrm{MoO}_{x}$ NSs exhibited promising OER performance with an overpotential of $276~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10.0\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , much lower than that of NiFe alloy $\mathrm{.}370\mathrm{mV}]$ and equal to $\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ $(275~\mathrm{~mV})$ . Moreover, the NiFe- $\mathrm{.}\mathrm{MoO}_{x}$ NSs exhibited a small
Tafel slope of $55~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}}$ , as well as a significantly enhanced stability. The excellent activity is owing to the high surface area, high porosity, as well as the increased valence state of surface Ni (Table 1).
Small molecule electrooxidation reaction (e.g., methanol, ethanol, hydrazine, formic acid, and glucose) is the heart process of direct liquid fuel cells, such as direct alcohol fuel cells, direct hydrazine hydrate fuel cells, and glucose biofuel cells, thus receiving tremendous attention.[122–125] However, the efficient oxidation of small molecules is still impeded by the high overpotential. Therefore, the design and preparation of high-performance electrocatalysts are important. Our group synthesized a hierarchical Pd-based heteronanostructure which exhibited excellent catalytic activity and robust durability toward ethanol electrooxidation reaction.[126] In this catalyst, ultrafine Pd nanoparticles (NPs) were uniformly anchored on the surface and interlayer of
CoAl-LDH nanowalls (Pd/LDH-NWs) via the surface- and space-confined synthesis, respectively. The resulting Pd/ LDH-NWs catalyst exhibited a high forward oxidation current density of $2.01\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and a reverse oxidation current density of $2.20\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , which exceeded those of the Pd/C (0.41 and $0.34\mathrm{~\mA~}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ) and Pd/LDH catalyst (0.51 and $0.55\mathrm{~mA~}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ (Figure  7d). The experimental and density functional theory (DFT) results confirm that there is a strong interaction between LDH and Pd which causes the electron transfer from LDH to Pd, thereby enhancing the activity toward ethanol oxidation reaction. Besides, by virtue of the surface-confinement effect of graphene, $\mathrm{Pd/rGO}$ electrocatalyst was synthesized for the electrooxidation of formic acid.[127] As shown in Figure 7e, the $\mathrm{Pd/rGO}$ exhibited a formic acid oxidation peak current of $32.1\mathrm{A}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , much higher than that of the commercial $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ catalyst. The enhanced electrooxidation activity for formic acid is due to the uniform dispersion of Pd NPs on rGO sheets that exposes more active sites. The $\mathtt{r G O}$ sheets also guarantee good electrical conductivity. In addition, recently, single atom electrocatalyst for hydrazine electrooxidation reaction was reported by Song and coworkers.[128] In their work, single ruthenium $(\mathrm{Ru})$ atoms were successfully supported on the monolayer NiFe-LDH $\mathrm{(Ru/LDH)}$ through the surface-confined synthesis strategy. The $\mathrm{Ru/LDH}$ exhibits a low potential of $1.26\mathrm{~V~}$ at the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ for hydrazine electrooxidation, which was 104 and $134~\mathrm{mV}$ lower than that of mono-NiFe and bulkNiFe (Figure  7f). To highlight, the single Ru atoms play an essential role in stabilizing the hydrazine electrooxidation intermediates and decreasing the band gap energy.
## 4.2. Electrocatalytic Reduction Reactions
The oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) is a key reaction occurring at the cathode in fuel cells and metal-air batteries, in which oxygen is reduced to water molecules or metal oxides (such as $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm O)$ .[134–138] Same as the OER and HER, considerable attention has been paid to explore nonprecious metal catalysts for ORR. Nitrogen-doped carbon materials have been proven as highly active ORR catalysts due to the activation of carbon atoms by electronegative nitrogen atoms, as reported by a number of reviews.[139,140] In this part, NC-based ORR catalysts synthesized via confined synthesis method were introduced and discussed. For instance, Shao and co-workers synthesized various 2D carbon-based ORR electrocatalysts via space-confined synthesis strategy by using MOF as the carbon precursors and LDH as the template. Among them, the 2D carbon-based network exhibited superior ORR performance with a positive onset potential $(0.94~\mathrm{V}$ vs RHE), a high limited current density $(5.5\ \mathrm{mA\cm^{-2}},$ , as well as a good stability $(\approx99\%$ current retention over 20 000 s) (Figure  8c).[73] The ORR performance was further enhanced by tuning the molar ratio of metal ions $(\mathrm{Co}/\mathrm{Zn})$ in the carbon precursors: the Co species promotes graphitic degree of carbon while Zn element facilitates the N-doping. The optimal nanostructured catalyst showed superior ORR activity with an ultrahigh onset potential of 0.976 V versus RHE and a limited current density of $5.8\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , superior to the commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst with an onset potential of $0.892\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE (Figure  8d).[140] In addition, Wan and co-workers reported the space-confined synthesis of pyridinic- and pyrrolic-nitrogendoped graphene (NG), which exhibited high activity and good stability for ORR in acidic electrolytes.[141] Due to the unique 2D confinement space of MMT, high-quantity planar pyridinic and pyrrolic $\mathrm{~N~}$ sites were generated on the NG (Figure  8e). This NG $\ @\mathrm{H}$ -MMT catalyst showed promising ORR activity with a positive half-wave potential (only $60~\mathrm{mV}$ less than that of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ catalyst) and an ultralow $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ yield of $2.2\%$ at all potentials (Figure 8f). Besides, Feng and co-workers prepared high-performance carbon-based ORR catalyst by surface-confined growth of nitrogen-rich polymer on 4-iodophenyl-functionalized $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ nanoplates, followed by a pyrolysis process.[142] This 2D porous carbon hybrid material displayed superior ORR performances with a positive half-wave potential of $-0.14\mathrm{~V~}$ versus $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ , $108\mathrm{mV}$ lower than that of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ -free sample, and only $11\mathrm{mV}$ higher than the value of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ , impressively demonstrating its high catalytic activity. The good ORR activity could be ascribed to the hierarchical 2D porous structure of the nitrogen-doped carbon that promoted the diffusion of ion and gas. Furthermore, the same group also developed a high-performance iron/nitrogen co-doping carbon-based ORR electrocatalyst by using a surface-confined reaction between ferrocene and rGO.[143] After the pyrolysis and ammonia activation of the cyclopentadienyliron (CpFe)-modified rGO at $800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15\ \mathrm{min}$ , the as-obtained catalyst exhibited good electrochemical performance with a low half-wave potential of $-0.29\mathrm{~V~}$ versus $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ . It also showed a high diffusionlimiting current density of $4.86~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and stable methanol tolerance.
## 5. Conclusions and Perspectives
In summary, recent advances in the confined synthesis of 2D nanostructured materials by using layered materials as “2D nanoreactors” are reviewed. The layered materials have been proven as promising nanoreactors to confine and transform reagents to the targeted nanomaterials. By virtue of space-confined and surfaceconfined syntheses, a number of well-defined 2D nanomaterials, including metal NSs or nanohybrids, transition metal oxide and sulfide NSs, nanostructured carbon materials, polymers, MOFs, COFs, and PCHs have been successfully synthesized. These asprepared 2D nanomaterials have shown promising potentials in energy-related applications. For instance, the 2D LDHs and 2D noble metal composites have been demonstrated as excellent electrocatalysts toward OER and small molecules oxidation. Moreover, the 2D carbon nanomaterials, MOFs and TMDs exhibit promising electrocatalytic activities toward the HER and ORR. Confined synthesis of 2D nanomaterials with controllable compositions and morphologies has become a rapidly growing research area, which paves a new pathway to attain fascinating structures and functionalities.
Although significant progress has been made in the field of confined synthesis of 2D nanomaterials, challenges still remain. First, from the perspective of new materials exploration, a large number of functional 2D materials can be synthesized by using various nanoreactors and reaction precursors. For instance, 2D $\mathrm{MoS}_{2}$ and Au NSs have been successfully prepared via confined synthesis in nanoreactors. Inspired by this strategy, other 2D TMDs $(\mathrm{W}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{TaS}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{TiS}_{2}$ , $\tt W S e_{2}$ , $\mathrm{MoSe}_{2}$ , etc.) and 2D metals (Pt, Pd, Ir, Ru, Ag, etc.) could also be explored by reasonably selecting the reaction precursors and layered materials. In particular, if 2D nanomaterials with unusual crystal phases[144–148] or amorphous structures,[149–151] and crystal-phase heterostructures[152–155] or amorphous/crystalline hetero-phase nanostructures[156,157] can be prepared via confined synthesis, their promising properties and applications can be explored, which is an important research direction in the near future. Moreover, other host matrices, such as black phosphorus,[158,159] hexagonal boron nitride,[160,161] graphitic carbon nitride,[162,163] silicene,[164,165] antimonene,[166,167] 2D perovskites,[168,169] as well as 2D MOFs and COFs, could be developed as new nano­ reactors. In addition, mature confined synthesis process with high-yield, high-purity and scalable production is highly desired to ultimately realize the practical applications of 2D nanostructured materials.
Second, in comparison with the traditional synthesis methods in liquid and gaseous environment, the mechanism and growth process of confined synthesis still need to be studied. Highend characterization techniques, such as in situ FTIR, in situ Raman, in situ X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) spectroscopy, as well as in situ TEM, are highly required to monitor the crystal growth, monomer polymerization and topological transformation in nanoreactors. It will provide guidance or direct evidence to comprehensively understand the new reaction kinetic and thermodynamic processes within a confined microenvironment, which will benefit the rational design of “nanoreactors” and “reactants” to obtain the “target products.” This is undoubtedly dependent on the development of combined instruments and in situ or operando characterization methods.
Third, the energy storage and catalytic performances are strongly dependent on the structural characteristics of nanomaterials. By modulating the microenvironment of nanoreactors, the structures, compositions and morphologies of 2D nanomaterials can be finely controlled/tuned. For instance, 2D nanoreactors take the merits of tunable interlayer spacing, abundant exposed surface atoms, and unique surface chemical states. A detailed understanding of these structural parameters and their effects on the resulting 2D nanomaterials will give instructions on the confined synthesis of target products with specific structure and functionality. For instance, a “co-confinement strategy” through co-intercalation of guest with target species into 2D nanoreactors can be used to precisely control the heteroatom-doping, planes, edges, and defects of the obtained nanomaterials, so as to improve their electrocatalytic activities toward reactions such as OER, ORR, and HER. In addition, other applications of these well-organized 2D nanomaterials, such as photovoltaic cells,[170] photocatalysis,[171] gas storage and separation,[172] biosensing,[173] and controlled drug release,[174] still need in-depth exploration.
Along with the digestion of the present achievements, we believe that the confined synthesis of 2D nanomaterials based on layered materials will be developed into an important synthesis protocol at molecular level, and will undoubtedly have a promising future. With a profound understanding of the new reaction mechanism within a restricted microenvironment, the confined synthesis strategy would serve as an effective approach to prepare more functional nanomaterials with expected structures and tunable properties, which would open up an important research direction for the precisely controlled synthesis of advanced materials with broad applications in energy storage/conversion.
